### New Helper: `ReadyNoBlockedAncestor()`

Add a new helper function in `internal/cli/query_helpers.go` that returns a NOT EXISTS subquery using a recursive CTE to walk the ancestor chain:

```sql
NOT EXISTS (
    WITH RECURSIVE ancestors(id) AS (
        SELECT parent FROM tasks WHERE id = t.id AND parent IS NOT NULL
        UNION ALL
        SELECT t2.parent FROM tasks t2
        JOIN ancestors a ON t2.id = a.id
        WHERE t2.parent IS NOT NULL
    )
    SELECT 1 FROM ancestors a
    JOIN dependencies d ON d.task_id = a.id
    JOIN tasks blocker ON blocker.id = d.blocked_by
    WHERE blocker.status NOT IN ('done', 'cancelled')
)
```

The CTE collects all ancestor IDs by walking `parent` pointers up to the root. The outer query then checks whether any ancestor has an unclosed dependency blocker.

### Integration Points

**`ReadyConditions()`** â€” Add `ReadyNoBlockedAncestor()` as the 4th condition. All consumers (`list --ready`, `tick ready`, stats) automatically pick up the change since they compose from `ReadyConditions()`.

**`BlockedConditions()`** â€” Add the EXISTS inverse to the OR clause:
```sql
OR EXISTS (
    WITH RECURSIVE ancestors(id) AS ( ... )
    SELECT 1 FROM ancestors a
    JOIN dependencies d ON d.task_id = a.id
    JOIN tasks blocker ON blocker.id = d.blocked_by
    WHERE blocker.status NOT IN ('done', 'cancelled')
)
```

This follows the existing pattern: each concern is a separate helper, composed into conditions.

### Edge Case: Closed Ancestors in the Chain

Don't stop walking at closed/cancelled ancestors. A closed parent with open children is an inconsistency that shouldn't occur in practice, and stopping early adds complexity for no benefit. The CTE walks unconditionally to the root.©work_unit¢v1©work_type¤epic¥phase­specification¥topic¶blocked-ancestor-readyªconfidence¤high«source_fileÙ]/Users/leeovery/Code/tick/.workflows/v1/specification/blocked-ancestor-ready/specification.md©timestampÏ␀␀ßİ ,©embeddingÜ␀Ë?‹0␀␀␀␀␀Ë?D␀␀␀␀␀Ë?¹œ␀␀␀␀␀Ë?l`␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿”ü␀␀␀␀␀Ë?„Ø␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿MD␀␀␀␀␀Ë?“0␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿\␀␀␀␀␀Ë?§␀␀␀␀␀Ë?œğ␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿„8␀␀␀␀␀Ë?r␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿Y,␀␀␀␀␀Ë?•␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿“h␀␀␀␀␀Ë?{´␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿Wp␀␀␀␀␀Ë?¡ü␀␀␀␀␀Ë?®È␀␀␀␀␀Ë?°␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿¢`␀␀␀␀␀Ë?ªğ␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿”␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿LĞ␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿“à␀␀␀␀␀Ë?“`␀␀␀␀␀Ë? T␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿t␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿˜4␀␀␀␀␀Ë?•␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿•␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿¥␀␀␀␀␀␀Ë?¥␀␀␀␀␀␀Ë?£¬␀␀␀␀␀Ë?z|␀␀␀␀␀Ë?Œ0␀␀␀␀␀Ë?š\␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿s@␀␀␀␀␀Ë?Ÿ␀␀␀␀␀Ë? D␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿¡0␀␀␀␀␀Ë?t␀␀␀␀␀Ë?§L␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿§Ü␀␀␀␀␀Ë?”␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿Vø␀␀␀␀␀Ë?•´␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿w€␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿‚␀␀␀␀␀Ë?‹€␀␀␀␀␀Ë?˜4␀␀␀␀␀Ë?r(␀␀␀␀␀Ë?„¬␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿Œ␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿¤H␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿˜8␀␀␀␀␀Ë?qx␀␀␀␀␀Ë?‘l␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿“Ø␀␀␀␀␀Ë?„”␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿ø␀␀␀␀␀Ë?e°␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿Œ ␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿Sà␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿r(␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿›´␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿˜ˆ␀␀␀␀␀Ë?␀␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿•”␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿q˜␀␀␀␀␀Ë?º␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿¤Ô␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿ Ü␀␀␀␀␀Ë?ˆx␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿t␀␀␀␀␀Ë?¤X␀␀␀␀␀Ë?}Ø␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿‘€␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿—␀␀␀␀␀Ë?œL␀␀␀␀␀Ë?…,␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿…„␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿œ␀␀␀␀␀Ë?b@␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿(␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿” ␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿„´␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿¨␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿˜ô␀␀␀␀␀Ë?¨␀␀␀␀␀Ë? x␀␀␀␀␀Ë?␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿‚(␀␀␀␀␀Ë?—@␀␀␀␀␀Ë?ˆ¨␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿4L␀␀␀␀␀Ë?§t␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿wH␀␀␀␀␀Ë?®␀␀␀␀␀Ë?“à␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿·$␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿yœ␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿®¼␀␀␀␀␀Ë?±␀␀␀␀␀Ë?š␀␀␀␀␀Ë?pt␀␀␀␀␀Ë?␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿rP␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿£,␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿°t␀␀␀␀␀Ë?~À␀␀␀␀␀Ë?£œ␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿’d␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿—ü␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿« ␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿¥è␀␀␀␀␀Ë?ƒˆ␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿¤H␀␀␀␀␀Ë?¨˜␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿W˜␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿1È␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿’„␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿„à␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿–¨␀␀␀␀␀Ë?˜„␀␀␀␀␀Ë?¥¼␀␀␀␀␀Ë?„H␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿Œ¬␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿{P␀␀␀␀␀Ë?à␀␀␀␀␀Ë?¨`␀␀␀␀␀Ë?¢`␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿|<␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿t␀␀␀␀␀Ë?Ÿ|␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿±␀␀␀␀␀␀Ë?…¨␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿£Ä␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿š␀␀␀␀␀Ë?…ü␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿rt␀␀␀␀␀Ë?«T␀␀␀␀␀Ë?hĞ␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿␀␀␀␀␀Ë?/␀␀␀␀␀Ë?}␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿“␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿„ˆ␀␀␀␀␀Ë?‚x␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿†Ø␀␀␀␀␀Ë?›˜␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿ l␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿Œ␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿«␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿¢ô␀␀␀␀␀Ë?À␀␀␀␀␀Ë?¢ô␀␀␀␀␀Ë?™È␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿–ø␀␀␀␀␀Ë?›l␀␀␀␀␀Ë?–ä␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿¢␀␀␀␀␀Ë?™„␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿›ğ␀␀␀␀␀Ë?¤ ␀␀␀␀␀Ë?LX␀␀␀␀␀Ë?•p␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿°␀␀␀␀␀Ë¿iŒ␀␀␀␀␀Ë?‚p␀␀